{"id":"h158","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"claim","question":"Is the following claim true? Pluto was discovered by Clyde Tombaugh.","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"author-written for this project; verified by the author","claims":[{"text":"Clyde Tombaugh discovered Pluto in February 1930 at Lowell Observatory in Arizona.","confidence":0.97,"lean":0.9,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Tombaugh identified Pluto by comparing photographic plates taken nights apart and spotting a moving object.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.8,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The IAU and standard historical accounts credit Tombaugh with the discovery.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.9,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The observatory announced the discovery in March 1930.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.4,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The search was driven by Percival Lowell's Planet X predictions, so the effort was not Tombaugh's alone.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Pluto was photographed in 1915 by Slipher's team and earlier by Lowell staff, but not recognized as a planet.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Pluto's discovery was the product of a longer institutional effort, not a single moment.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The counterpoints do not change who is credited; they only provide context.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.8,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Tombaugh identified Pluto as a moving object and confirmed it, which is the standard meaning of 'discovered by.'","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.9,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":-0.4,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":-0.3,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":-0.3,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.5,"relati